.page truncate 1 shrink or extend a file to a given size

# SYNOPSIS
*truncate* *-s* [*+*|*-*]_SIZE_[*K*|*M*|*G*] [*-c*] _FILE_ ...

# DESCRIPTION
`truncate` sets the size of each _FILE_. A file that is made shorter loses its tail; one that is made longer is padded with zero bytes. A file that does not exist is created, unless *-c* is given. _SIZE_ is a number of bytes, optionally followed by `K`, `M` or `G` (powers of 1024). With a leading `+` or `-` the size is relative to the current size of the file; a result below zero becomes zero.

# OPTIONS
@ *-s* _SIZE_
    The size to set (required).
@ *-c*
    Do not create files that do not exist.

# EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 if a file could not be opened or truncated, or the size is not valid.

# EXAMPLES
    truncate -s 10M disk.img
    truncate -s -1K log.txt

# SEE ALSO
dd(1), ls(1)
